No, it should not. Here's why:
```C#
public class AJsonConverter : JsonConverter<A>
{
    public override bool CanConvert(Type t) => true;
}
```
Clearly, having a converter constrained to type `A` should not provide means for the converter to say "_Hey, i am good for converting type `FooBar`. Oh, i can also convert `int`. I'm excellent like that...!_" Tell me, how would you pass an existing `int` or a `FooBar` instance into the converter's ReadJson/WriteJson methods then? I mean, just take a look at the method signatures of the overridable `JsonConverter<T>` ReadJson/WriteJson methods. That's why the method is sealed.

Well, you could argue that after determining that a given type is derived from `A`, `CanConvert` could invoke another (protected) overridable method that determines if that given derived type is applicable to the converter -- and there would be merit to that argument. However, i would encourage you to peruse the commit history of the project -- you might notice that there is/was only a minuscule development activity in the recent years, so in my subjective opinion it is very unlikely that any new feature or feature enhancement will see the light of day...

As to your problem, `JsonConverter<T>` is a **convenience** class on top of the non-generic `JsonConverter`. If the generic `JsonConverter<T>` doesn't suit your specific needs, use the non-generic JsonConverter as basis for your custom converter.

I don't believe your example sufficiently justifies why the method must be sealed. A more reasonable approach would be to treat such cases as incorrect implementations rather than restricting the API. After all, users can always provide wrong implementations for any method if they choose to. like
```cs
class FooJsonConverter:JsonConverter  {
   CanRead  =>  true;
   ReadJson(...)  => throw new NotImplementedException();
}
```

You can either manually convert the hierarchical `JToken` representation into the target types of interests in a recursive manner.

Or, you can leverage a simple yet somewhat tricky JsonConverter to do the job (which will be used by a `JsonSerializer` instance passed to the `ToObject` method):
```C#
public class ToObjectConverter : JsonConverter
{
    private bool _isConverterToObjectCall = false;

    public override bool CanConvert(Type objectType)
    {
        if (!_isConverterToObjectCall)
            return objectType == typeof(object);

        _isConverterToObjectCall = false;
        return false;
    }

    public override object? ReadJson(JsonReader reader, Type objectType, object? existingValue, JsonSerializer serializer)
    {
        var jt = JToken.ReadFrom(reader);

        //
        // If the target type is System.Object and the JToken is either a json array or a json object,
        // change the target type to object[] or Dictionary<string, object>, respectively.
        //
        if (objectType == typeof(object))
        {
            objectType = jt.Type switch
            {
                JTokenType.Object => typeof(Dictionary<string, object>),
                JTokenType.Array => typeof(object[]),
                _ => objectType
            };
        }

        //
        // This boolean flag prevents this JsonConverter to be applied to the token we just read.
        // Otherwise this JsonConverter.Read method will be called again and again on the same json token/value
        // recursively until a StackOverflowException puts an end to all things...
        //
        _isConverterToObjectCall = true;

        var o = jt.ToObject(objectType, serializer);
        return o;
    }

    public override void WriteJson(JsonWriter writer, object? value, JsonSerializer serializer)
        => throw new NotImplementedException();
}
```

To use this converter with the `JToken.ToObject` method, configure a `JsonSerializer` with this converter and pass it to the ToObject method. One possible way to do it (but not just the only way to do it):
```C#
var jsonSerializer = new JsonSerializer();
jsonSerializer.Converters.Add(new ToObjectConverter());
var m = jObjectInstance.ToObject<Model>(jsonSerializer);

public record Model(object[] SomeArray);
```

Note that the logic of the `_isConverterToObjectCall` flag in the json converter above relies on the fact that the serializer will call the `CanConvert` method again for each json value it encounters, even if it has been already called for the same type before. This is an undocumented implementation detail of Newtonsoft.Json. Since i am not involved in development nor maintenance of the Newtonsoft.Json library, i can't guarantee that future versions of Newtonsoft.Json will behave in exactly the manner the `_isConverterToObjectCall` flag logic relies upon.

Also due to the implementation of the `_isConverterToObjectCall` flag (which isn't thread-safe), the same `ToObjectConverter` instance must not be used by concurrent deserialization jobs.

Disclaimer: The JsonConverter implementation above is _illustrative_. It hasn't been exhaustively tested and might contain silly coding errors. It's up to you to adapt it to your needs...

