I could come up with a hack for this - not sure if it will work under any circumstances. But if someone is struggeling with this like me I will share it.

The idea is to make Json.NET think that the read-only property is writable so it will not be added to JsonExtensionData by use of a ContractResolver:

```
 public class CustomContractResolver: DefaultContractResolver
    {

        protected override JsonProperty CreateProperty(MemberInfo member, MemberSerialization memberSerialization)
        {
            var property = base.CreateProperty(member, memberSerialization);

            if (!property.Writable)
            {
                property.ValueProvider = new MakeReadOnlyPropertyWritableValueProvider(member);
                property.Writable = true;
            }

            property.ShouldDeserialize = o => property.Writable;

            return property;
        }
    }

    public class MakeReadOnlyPropertyWritableValueProvider : IValueProvider
    {
        private readonly ReflectionValueProvider _reflectionValueProvider;

        public MakeReadOnlyPropertyWritableValueProvider(MemberInfo memberInfo)
        {
            _reflectionValueProvider = new ReflectionValueProvider(memberInfo);
        }

        public void SetValue(object target, object? value)
        {
        }

        public object GetValue(object target)
        {
            return _reflectionValueProvider.GetValue(target);
        }
    }
```

The `MakeReadOnlyPropertyWritableValueProvider` is used for all non writabel properties. It simply ignores the SetValue call, because the property is not acutally writable. But Json.Net thinks so. The GetValue-call is delegated to the Json.NET ReflectionValueProvider so all the godness being there will remain.

I feel this is a hacky solution, but it works without the need to change your contracts.

